{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## MERGED — and PROVEN still un-applied. Ticket stays OPEN.\n\nGerrit change **1430** merged to `main` at 09:28 UTC as **24ec1e6d8a**, with `LLM-Review +1` (PASS,\n6 advisory / 0 blocking) and `Verified +1` (CI run 30991721069, all jobs success). Confirmed on a\nclean detached worktree at merged `origin/main`: both alarms carry\n`alarm_actions = [aws_sns_topic.alerts.arn]`, and the new guard is 5/5 green there.\n\n## The apply caveat is not theoretical — I measured it AFTER the merge\n\nImmediately after submit, read-only `describe-alarms`:\n\n    rebar-gerrit-replication-errors   AlarmActions: []   OKActions: []\n    rebar-gerrit-voter-errors         AlarmActions: []   OKActions: []\n\n**Still empty. Merging changed nothing in production.** This is bug\n[rebar:1c39-96d4-8f9e-4d38] demonstrated live rather than argued: the HCL is on `main`, CI is\ngreen, the ticket looks done — and both alarms continue to notify nobody. Anyone reading only the\nmerge would conclude the safety net exists. It does not yet.\n\nI did not run the apply: it mutates shared AWS infrastructure, which is outside what I am\nauthorised to change here, and this ticket designates apply as operator-run. To be precise about\nthe blocker — AWS credentials ARE present (account 896586841071, user joe_frontier), so this is a\nmatter of authority and blast radius, not missing access.\n\n## Status of the acceptance criteria\n\nDONE by the merge:\n- [x] `voter_errors` declares `alarm_actions` AND `ok_actions` -> `aws_sns_topic.alerts`.\n- [x] `replication_errors` (the sibling) does the same, in the SAME change.\n- [x] guard test parses every alarm block and fails on any lacking `alarm_actions`; seen RED first\n      (naming exactly those two), GREEN after; mutation-checked both ways.\n- [x] anti-vacuity assertion present AND demonstrated to fire (it caught a real zero-match parser\n      bug during authoring).\n- [x] explicit `# rebar:allow-actionless-alarm: <reason>` opt-out required; silence never passes.\n- [x] `monitoring_1fa8.tf`'s 'UNLIKE S4b it WIRES SNS' comment corrected.\n- [x] audit recorded: 13 alarms, exactly 2 were silent, both now fixed; no alarm is created\n      outside `infra/terraform/`, so the guard covers the whole surface.\n\nSTILL OPEN — these are why this ticket is NOT being closed:\n- [ ] the change is APPLIED and live `describe-alarms` shows non-empty `AlarmActions`/`OKActions`.\n- [ ] one FORCED notification observed end-to-end (delivery, not configuration).\n\n## Operator steps to actually finish this\n\n    terraform -chdir=infra/terraform apply     # expect EXACTLY 2 alarms changed, no other diff\n    aws cloudwatch describe-alarms --alarm-names rebar-gerrit-voter-errors rebar-gerrit-replication-errors \\\n      --query 'MetricAlarms[].{n:AlarmName,a:AlarmActions}'\n    aws cloudwatch set-alarm-state --alarm-name rebar-gerrit-voter-errors \\\n      --state-value ALARM --state-reason 9baf-delivery-proof\n    # confirm the email ACTUALLY ARRIVED at the subscriber, then let it settle back to OK\n\nNote for the Bedrock cutover [rebar:eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a], which leans on this alarm as its\npre-flight safety net: until that apply runs, the net is still HCL only."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785922146285914001,"uuid":"dc2b942f-2d9f-47ef-a610-aaa2eac56882"}